 ## Overview
-Frequency Domain Volume Rendering (FD method) is a direct volume rendering technique that performs resampling (zooming and shearing) in the frequency domain using Fourier transforms to avoid blurring artifacts associated with spatial domain interpolation, particularly for large zoom factors [S1]. It exploits signal processing principles such as the shifting, packing, and derivative theorems to achieve the best theoretical reconstruction quality using the ideal sinc filter [S1]. Unlike standard spatial domain methods (e.g., B-spline, Catmull-Rom) or hybrid approaches that zoom the intermediate image, this method resamples volume slices in object space via zero-padding, ensuring superior accuracy for high-magnification views [S1].
+Frequency Domain Volume Rendering (FD method) is a direct volume rendering technique that performs resampling (zooming and shearing) in the frequency domain using Fourier transforms to avoid blurring artifacts associated with spatial domain interpolation, particularly for large zoom factors [S1]. It exploits signal processing principles such as the shifting, packing, and derivative theorems to achieve the best theoretical reconstruction quality using the ideal sinc filter [S1]. Unlike standard spatial domain methods (e.g., B-spline, Catmull-Rom) or hybrid approaches that zoom the intermediate image, this method resamples volume slices in the frequency domain via zero-padding, ensuring superior accuracy for high-magnification views [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method integrates frequency-domain resampling into the Shear-Warp factorization pipeline. It transforms the volume to sheared object space, composites slices into an intermediate image, and applies a final warping step.
